00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:49.716
to follow up on that.

00:03:49.716 --> 00:03:52.090
But there's some other angles
of thinking about this too,

00:03:52.090 --> 00:03:53.740
not just kind of
straight product-centric.

00:03:53.740 --> 00:03:55.840
So we'll revisit the where
should I run my code,

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:58.390
and then go into next steps.

00:03:58.390 --> 00:04:01.270
But this is kind of
like this big decision.

00:04:01.270 --> 00:04:03.950
Like, we're going to spend
the whole talk on how

00:04:03.950 --> 00:04:05.540
to make this one decision.

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:07.630
And I'll get this
back at the end,

00:04:07.630 --> 00:04:10.360
but this is a relatively
low-risk decision.

00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:11.570
You can move things around.

00:04:11.570 --> 00:04:13.154
You can mix and match.

00:04:13.154 --> 00:04:14.570
And there's a lot
of options here.

00:04:14.570 --> 00:04:17.709
So no worries on--

00:04:17.709 --> 00:04:19.209
it's a low risk.

00:04:19.209 --> 00:04:21.279
Let's start with Compute Engine.

00:04:21.279 --> 00:04:24.450
And Compute Engine
is virtual machines

00:04:24.450 --> 00:04:25.590
like at a very high level.

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:26.950
It's virtual machines, network.

00:04:26.950 --> 00:04:31.180
You get to inherit the
network the Google Cloud

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:32.720
platform is built on.

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:35.960
And what makes these virtual
machines special, in a way,

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:38.170
is that they're built
in Google data centers.

00:04:38.170 --> 00:04:41.770
They live, essentially,
on the internet.

00:04:41.770 --> 00:04:43.720
And the interconnect
between those machines

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:46.480
is very, very fast.

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:52.810
In numbers, these are very,
potentially, large machines.

00:04:52.810 --> 00:04:55.180
So you can go up to 64 CPUs.

00:04:55.180 --> 00:04:59.790
You can go up to 416 gigs of
ram, 64 terabytes of disk.

00:04:59.790 --> 00:05:04.000
We now have GPUs, if that's of
interest for your workloads.

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:08.039
And all of these pieces are
independently configurable.

00:05:08.039 --> 00:05:09.580
So you can mix, and
match, and create

00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:12.060
the machine shape that's
right for your workloads.

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:14.260
So especially for a lot
of existing workloads,

00:05:14.260 --> 00:05:15.240
it's hard to tune them.

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:17.861
They're kind of like
a certain shape.

00:05:17.861 --> 00:05:19.360
And if you're in
that situation, you

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:21.994
can kind of create machines that
are the right shape and size.

00:05:21.994 --> 00:05:23.410
So this is, you
could put whatever

00:05:23.410 --> 00:05:26.800
size disk on whatever size
instance, that sort of thing.

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:29.580
The one constraint is
there's a ram to CPU ratio.

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:32.230
They're a little bit connected.

00:05:32.230 --> 00:05:33.650
These start relatively quickly.

00:05:33.650 --> 00:05:36.820
So you go, in about 30 seconds,
you're running your code.

00:05:36.820 --> 00:05:39.430
So the machine gets
provisioned, boots,

00:05:39.430 --> 00:05:43.481
and gets off into your boot
scripts and your software.

00:05:43.481 --> 00:05:44.980
We have a bunch of
pre-built images.

00:05:44.980 --> 00:05:47.570
And it's virtualized hardware.

00:05:47.570 --> 00:05:52.840
So you can take your own images
from other places and use them.

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:56.020
Lots of different options
for Linux OSs, and also

00:05:56.020 --> 00:05:58.060
Windows Server.

00:05:58.060 --> 00:05:59.710
So got that there.

00:05:59.710 --> 00:06:02.560
I'm going to dive down
deep in just one part,

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:05.817
just to give you an example
of how rich this really is.

00:06:05.817 --> 00:06:07.400
And there's a few
Compute Engine talks

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:08.566
I'll recommend you to later.

00:06:08.566 --> 00:06:11.030
But just to dive deep
for just a moment,

00:06:11.030 --> 00:06:12.400
the disks here are amazing.

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:16.310
So you get a slice of an entire
data center worth of disks.

00:06:16.310 --> 00:06:18.070
So while you're
provisioning something

00:06:18.070 --> 00:06:19.600
that is a spinning
disk-- you know,

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:21.940
I'd like a terabyte of disk.

00:06:21.940 --> 00:06:24.130
That's actually on hundreds
or thousands of disks

00:06:24.130 --> 00:06:25.610
out in the data center.

00:06:25.610 --> 00:06:27.550
And so when you
make a read, we pull

00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:29.860
from a couple different
places, and actually,

00:06:29.860 --> 00:06:31.690
bring that back up to you.

00:06:31.690 --> 00:06:34.900
So you get very
consistent performance.

00:06:34.900 --> 00:06:36.850
And the random
read is very good.

00:06:36.850 --> 00:06:38.800
You can pick whatever
size you need.

00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:41.350
And network-attached
storage is our default.

00:06:41.350 --> 00:06:43.150
That's what we boot
all the machines on.

00:06:43.150 --> 00:06:45.250
That's how most of our
internal workloads work.

00:06:45.250 --> 00:06:48.432
We're really big fans of
network-attached storage.

00:06:48.432 --> 00:06:50.890
There are some cases where you
just need the really, really

00:06:50.890 --> 00:06:52.450
low latency IOPS.

00:06:52.450 --> 00:06:56.057
And so we also offer
machine-attached SSD

00:06:56.057 --> 00:06:56.890
for those scenarios.

00:06:56.890 --> 00:06:59.440
But most of our workloads,
most of the workloads

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:03.160
we see people use, use the
network-attached storage.

00:07:03.160 --> 00:07:05.770
And they're independent
of the VM lifecycle.

00:07:05.770 --> 00:07:09.200
So you can detach one from a
running VM if it's a data disk,

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:10.660
attach it to another one.

00:07:10.660 --> 00:07:13.302
You can make snapshots of
them while they're running.

00:07:13.302 --> 00:07:14.510
You can take those snapshots.

00:07:14.510 --> 00:07:16.040
And you can create
new disks from them,

00:07:16.040 --> 00:07:17.800
including in completely
different regions

00:07:17.800 --> 00:07:19.469
around the world.

00:07:19.469 --> 00:07:21.760
So if you're thinking of
things from an IT perspective,

00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:24.250
and you need to image a box,
it's just a button click,

00:07:24.250 --> 00:07:25.690
or API call.

00:07:25.690 --> 00:07:28.510
And then you can make that
available somewhere else.

00:07:28.510 --> 00:07:31.890
One gotcha as you're
evaluating the disks,

00:07:31.890 --> 00:07:35.185
larger disks have
higher throughput.

00:07:35.185 --> 00:07:37.060
So you get a bigger
slice of the data center.

00:07:37.060 --> 00:07:41.530
And so you get more throughput,
and higher IOP quotas.

00:07:41.530 --> 00:07:44.680
So if you're evaluating this,
there's some dock on it.

00:07:44.680 --> 00:07:46.770
But pay attention
to the disk size.

00:07:46.770 --> 00:07:48.514
OK, so I'll pull
back up a level.

00:07:48.514 --> 00:07:49.930
Most to talk is
not at this level.

00:07:49.930 --> 00:07:53.662
But the whole
platform is like this.

00:07:53.662 --> 00:07:55.870
Everywhere you look, there's
all kinds of good things

00:07:55.870 --> 00:07:57.790
that are there to
make your life easier,

00:07:57.790 --> 00:07:59.620
as you're operating
these systems,

00:07:59.620 --> 00:08:01.120
and as you're running
your software.

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:03.100
So there's that.

00:08:03.100 --> 00:08:05.230
And then, it's more than
just virtual machines.

00:08:05.230 --> 00:08:09.250
So this is kind of
an orchestration

00:08:09.250 --> 00:08:13.360
system for virtual computers.

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:16.570
So one example-- actually,
somebody I talked to earlier

00:08:16.570 --> 00:08:19.984
was saying they want to run
containers on virtual machines.

00:08:19.984 --> 00:08:22.150
And you may have like a
one-to-one-- a lot of times,

00:08:22.150 --> 00:08:24.340
it's really convenient
just to run one container

00:08:24.340 --> 00:08:25.150
on and one machine.

00:08:25.150 --> 00:08:27.662
You don't really want a
big orchestration system.

00:08:27.662 --> 00:08:29.620
So you can actually do
that straight to the UI,

00:08:29.620 --> 00:08:31.030
and point it at an image.

00:08:31.030 --> 00:08:33.280
And we'll start a
managed OS, and just

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:36.710
run your one container for you
if that's what you want to do.

00:08:36.710 --> 00:08:37.890
So that's an option.

00:08:37.890 --> 00:08:39.640
There's also groups
of all these machines.

00:08:39.640 --> 00:08:42.419
And this is where it
gets really powerful.

00:08:42.419 --> 00:08:44.662
One computer is only so big.

00:08:44.662 --> 00:08:46.120
But if you can
think of your system

00:08:46.120 --> 00:08:49.390
as a collection of
computing devices,

00:08:49.390 --> 00:08:51.592
the data center
is your computer.

00:08:51.592 --> 00:08:53.050
Then you can go
much, much further.

00:08:53.050 --> 00:08:56.890
And so we have something we call
managed instance groups, where

00:08:56.890 --> 00:08:59.440
you have a template for what
you want a machine to look like,

00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:01.210
either based off of
a snapshot of a disk,

00:09:01.210 --> 00:09:03.040
or a machine you've
pre-populated,

00:09:03.040 --> 00:09:05.470
or a script that sets them up.

00:09:05.470 --> 00:09:08.214
And then you say, OK, I'd
like to run five of these.

00:09:08.214 --> 00:09:10.630
And I'd like them all to be
behind the same load balancer.

00:09:10.630 --> 00:09:13.900
And you have a dial to
make more or less of those.

00:09:13.900 --> 00:09:16.210
Or you can hook up an
auto-scalar based on load,

00:09:16.210 --> 00:09:20.110
or CPU usage, memory,
that sort of thing.

00:09:20.110 --> 00:09:22.750
That instance group
can actually span

00:09:22.750 --> 00:09:27.170
multiple zones, multiple failure
zones, within the same region.

00:09:27.170 --> 00:09:29.080
So if you have a
system where you

00:09:29.080 --> 00:09:32.470
can have multiple workers
that are all collaborating

00:09:32.470 --> 00:09:34.960
on getting the work
done, you can very easily

00:09:34.960 --> 00:09:38.890
have a very high
reliability by having those

00:09:38.890 --> 00:09:41.260
in different failure zones.

00:09:41.260 --> 00:09:43.150
And then with using
that, we can actually--

00:09:43.150 --> 00:09:46.180
when you make your base OS,
base image, or your software,

00:09:46.180 --> 00:09:48.760
you can actually roll them out
with this mechanism, as well,

00:09:48.760 --> 00:09:50.332
and roll them back.

00:09:50.332 --> 00:09:51.790
And then just to
kind of highlight,

00:09:51.790 --> 00:09:54.540
the load balancer is the data
center level load balancer.

00:09:54.540 --> 00:09:57.240
So we've got an article
online talking about this.

00:09:57.240 --> 00:09:59.470
But you can basically
go from zero

00:09:59.470 --> 00:10:02.712
to a million requests per
second with no pre-warming,

00:10:02.712 --> 00:10:03.670
no talking to somebody.

00:10:03.670 --> 00:10:05.410
You're configuring
the data center

00:10:05.410 --> 00:10:06.790
to do the load
balancing for you.

00:10:06.790 --> 00:10:10.100
That's all happening
off of your machines.

00:10:10.100 --> 00:10:12.480
So when you're thinking
in Compute Engine, when

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:14.980
you're thinking in
VMs, this is logically

00:10:14.980 --> 00:10:16.730
virtual infrastructure.

00:10:16.730 --> 00:10:19.430
So you're thinking about your
software, the operating systems

00:10:19.430 --> 00:10:22.900
it runs on, the size and
shape of those machines,

00:10:22.900 --> 00:10:25.240
and what the networking
looks like between them,

00:10:25.240 --> 00:10:26.390
the configuration there.

00:10:26.390 --> 00:10:29.595
So this is, as a software
developer, as an IT person,

00:10:29.595 --> 00:10:30.970
this is what you
think about when

00:10:30.970 --> 00:10:36.210
you're working at this level
of abstraction, these pieces.

00:10:36.210 --> 00:10:41.142
Constraints-wise, if you need to
adapt very, very quickly, like

00:10:41.142 --> 00:10:44.339
in a few seconds
to a burst of load,

00:10:44.339 --> 00:10:45.880
that may be a little
bit challenging.

00:10:45.880 --> 00:10:49.830
So each machine starts very
quickly, about 30 seconds.

00:10:49.830 --> 00:10:56.740
But it does take 30 seconds to
get up to running your code.

00:10:56.740 --> 00:10:57.880
Now that's per machine.

00:10:57.880 --> 00:10:59.350
You can run
thousands of machines

00:10:59.350 --> 00:11:01.300
in a few minutes, no problem.

00:11:01.300 --> 00:11:04.656
So if you need to do a
big batch job, no problem.

00:11:04.656 --> 00:11:06.280
But if you need very,
very quick stuff,

00:11:06.280 --> 00:11:08.200
you may need to break
things up a little bit.

00:11:08.200 --> 00:11:10.040
And it is a whole
computer, so you'll

00:11:10.040 --> 00:11:13.390
have to decide how you
want to do your updates.

00:11:13.390 --> 00:11:17.380
It's an incredibly good
fit for existing systems.

00:11:17.380 --> 00:11:20.170
The abstractions
model very closely

00:11:20.170 --> 00:11:22.810
to what we're used to seeing,
both in our own data centers

00:11:22.810 --> 00:11:25.190
and in other virtual
environments.

00:11:25.190 --> 00:11:27.010
So you can usually
move very quickly

00:11:27.010 --> 00:11:28.859
from software you have running.

00:11:28.859 --> 00:11:31.150
I mentioned before, this
one-to-one container scenario.

00:11:31.150 --> 00:11:32.000
Sometimes, you've
got a container.

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:33.400
You just want to run it.

00:11:33.400 --> 00:11:35.380
It's very convenient for that.

00:11:35.380 --> 00:11:39.880
If you need specific kernels,
licenses, other very particular

00:11:39.880 --> 00:11:42.940
criteria about your
operating environment,

00:11:42.940 --> 00:11:45.275
usually that'll push you
towards Compute Engine

00:11:45.275 --> 00:11:46.150
and virtual machines.

00:11:49.307 --> 00:11:50.430
And let's go from there.

00:11:50.430 --> 00:11:51.282
OK, it's demo time.

00:11:51.282 --> 00:11:51.990
Too much talking.

00:11:54.680 --> 00:11:59.220
And we will do live
demos, because scary.

00:11:59.220 --> 00:12:04.060
OK, so you may or
may not have seen it,

00:12:04.060 --> 00:12:07.540
but this is the web
console for Google Cloud.

00:12:07.540 --> 00:12:10.390
And this is the single most
important piece of that.

00:12:10.390 --> 00:12:13.260
This button is access
to all the other pieces.

00:12:13.260 --> 00:12:16.000
It might not have looked like
it, but this is important.

00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:19.810
OK, Computer Engine
is down here.

00:12:19.810 --> 00:12:21.394
And when you first
come down, you

00:12:21.394 --> 00:12:23.310
can see your virtual
machines, these instances

00:12:23.310 --> 00:12:23.950
groups that I mentioned.

00:12:23.950 --> 00:12:25.575
You can play with
disks, and snapshots,

00:12:25.575 --> 00:12:26.800
and all this other stuff.

00:12:26.800 --> 00:12:29.104
We're going to spend
just a couple of minutes

00:12:29.104 --> 00:12:29.770
looking at this.

00:12:29.770 --> 00:12:31.570
So we can make an instance.

00:12:31.570 --> 00:12:33.730
We can pick which part
of the world it's in--

00:12:33.730 --> 00:12:36.550
data centers in
Asia, Europe, the US.

00:12:36.550 --> 00:12:39.250
We've got a whole lists of
things coming online this year,

00:12:39.250 --> 00:12:45.830
more in Europe, more in Asia,
Australia, and more places.

00:12:45.830 --> 00:12:48.280
So I can go ahead
and pick a location.

00:12:48.280 --> 00:12:50.470
I can pick a kind
of preset shape.

00:12:50.470 --> 00:12:55.280
But I can also kind
of drag and drop.

00:12:55.280 --> 00:12:57.630
OK, I'd like a machine
with more CPU than memory,

00:12:57.630 --> 00:12:59.760
or more memory than CPU.

00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:03.990
I can pick a particular
number for no good reason,

00:13:03.990 --> 00:13:06.210
and make this very
awkwardly-shaped machine.

00:13:06.210 --> 00:13:12.640
Maybe I'll name it
answer, and do that.

00:13:12.640 --> 00:13:15.870
We have a bunch of
maintained by us images that

00:13:15.870 --> 00:13:21.240
have software pre-installed
to do updates of the OS

00:13:21.240 --> 00:13:25.200
in the background, and connect
to the cloud a little bit

00:13:25.200 --> 00:13:28.440
easier, so that the security
tokens are available to you,

00:13:28.440 --> 00:13:30.110
and things like that.

00:13:30.110 --> 00:13:31.470
So these are all here.

00:13:31.470 --> 00:13:34.720
You can change the size
of the boot disk there.

00:13:34.720 --> 00:13:37.120
And then you just hit go.

00:13:37.120 --> 00:13:42.090
So this is now being
created off in Google Cloud.

00:13:42.090 --> 00:13:44.250
So we've said we
want this much space.

00:13:44.250 --> 00:13:46.130
We want it to be
running this disk.

00:13:46.130 --> 00:13:51.360
And this web UI uses
the HTTP REST API,

00:13:51.360 --> 00:13:54.270
the same API that the
command line tools use,

00:13:54.270 --> 00:13:58.380
and that you can also use from
your applications directly,

00:13:58.380 --> 00:14:00.600
if you like.

00:14:00.600 --> 00:14:03.180
So this usually takes
about 30 seconds.

00:14:03.180 --> 00:14:04.110
There it is.

00:14:04.110 --> 00:14:05.570
It's up.

00:14:05.570 --> 00:14:09.110
And we'll click my favorite
button, which is SSH.

00:14:09.110 --> 00:14:11.634
And since I'm already logged
in on the web with OAuth,

00:14:11.634 --> 00:14:12.800
Google Cloud knows who I am.

00:14:12.800 --> 00:14:16.160
We can do-- kind of
pre-populate some SSH keys,

00:14:16.160 --> 00:14:19.550
and SSH directly
from the web, you're

00:14:19.550 --> 00:14:21.489
on a Chromebook
or wherever else.

00:14:21.489 --> 00:14:23.780
I think I'm going to have to
push questions to the end.

00:14:23.780 --> 00:14:25.910
I apologize.

00:14:25.910 --> 00:14:32.090
And we're on a real computer.

00:14:32.090 --> 00:14:32.970
And there we go.

00:14:32.970 --> 00:14:36.300
So that's end of that
little quick whirlwind demo.

00:14:36.300 --> 00:14:38.454
But hopefully, that
gives you a feel

00:14:38.454 --> 00:14:39.870
of what Compute
Engine looks like,

00:14:39.870 --> 00:14:42.280
and how quickly it reacts.

00:14:42.280 --> 00:14:44.810
So why might you choose this?

00:14:44.810 --> 00:14:47.340
Consistency-- so this
is kind of actually

00:14:47.340 --> 00:14:48.900
true across Google Cloud.

00:14:48.900 --> 00:14:52.860
We are very concerned about
our systems working well

00:14:52.860 --> 00:14:53.402
all the time.

00:14:53.402 --> 00:14:55.485
And when you have distributed
systems, the one way

00:14:55.485 --> 00:14:57.970
you do that is getting your
long tail latency down low.

00:14:57.970 --> 00:15:00.940
And you want very
consistent performance.

00:15:00.940 --> 00:15:04.320
So if you do your load testing
with 10 computers on one day,

00:15:04.320 --> 00:15:06.930
and a week later,
you run them again,

00:15:06.930 --> 00:15:10.650
you should see almost
exactly the same performance.

00:15:10.650 --> 00:15:13.290
Choose the right
VM size you want.

00:15:13.290 --> 00:15:15.680
We can actually
resize disks that are

00:15:15.680 --> 00:15:17.730
attached to running machines.

00:15:17.730 --> 00:15:21.375
And then you can go in and
run the commands so your file

00:15:21.375 --> 00:15:22.500
system can actually see it.

00:15:22.500 --> 00:15:25.220
And I'll bet almost
everybody in the room

00:15:25.220 --> 00:15:28.440
has run out of disk before
on a critical machine.

00:15:28.440 --> 00:15:30.662
So that's super useful.

00:15:30.662 --> 00:15:32.620
On the billing end, we
try to make this really,

00:15:32.620 --> 00:15:33.740
really easy to think about.

00:15:33.740 --> 00:15:35.114
There's an entire
talk on billing

00:15:35.114 --> 00:15:37.230
that I'd like to point you at.

00:15:37.230 --> 00:15:38.980
But we're trying to
keep it really simple.

00:15:38.980 --> 00:15:42.460
Basically, if you use it more,
you get a bigger discount.

00:15:42.460 --> 00:15:43.770
That's the fundamentals there.

00:15:43.770 --> 00:15:46.830
So if you use a certain machine
shape for a whole month,

00:15:46.830 --> 00:15:49.020
you automatically get a
big chunk of discount--

00:15:49.020 --> 00:15:50.970
30% to 40%.

00:15:50.970 --> 00:15:54.660
If you have batch jobs, you
can use preemptable machines.

00:15:54.660 --> 00:15:59.010
And they are far,
far less money than

00:15:59.010 --> 00:16:02.060
the actual regular machines.

00:16:02.060 --> 00:16:03.900
Then they, actually,
are automatically

00:16:03.900 --> 00:16:05.850
shut down at 24 hours.

00:16:05.850 --> 00:16:07.764
And they could be shut
down at any point.

00:16:07.764 --> 00:16:09.180
That's what the
preemptable means.

00:16:09.180 --> 00:16:10.388
And then we bill per minute--

00:16:10.388 --> 00:16:12.336
so a minimum of 10 minutes.

00:16:12.336 --> 00:16:13.710
And if you run it
for 12 minutes,

00:16:13.710 --> 00:16:16.000
you pay for 12 minutes
of that computer.

00:16:16.000 --> 00:16:18.060
So if you want to make
something faster, you can.

00:16:18.060 --> 00:16:19.768
And then I just want
to kind of highlight

00:16:19.768 --> 00:16:22.140
that this architecture is
what most of us are used to.

00:16:22.140 --> 00:16:25.240
It's a really good fit
for most existing systems.

00:16:25.240 --> 00:16:28.320
So that's Compute Engine.

00:16:28.320 --> 00:16:29.662
We have multiple talks today.

00:16:29.662 --> 00:16:31.120
Or you can check
these out online--

00:16:31.120 --> 00:16:32.760
or and tomorrow
throughout the week.

00:16:32.760 --> 00:16:35.340
And you can check
these out online later.

00:16:35.340 --> 00:16:39.910
SQL Server on Compute Engine,
more Windows overall things,

00:16:39.910 --> 00:16:43.050
how to kind of scale really
big, and do updates and things

00:16:43.050 --> 00:16:45.090
with the managed
instance groups,

00:16:45.090 --> 00:16:47.190
saving money, which
is the billing talk,

00:16:47.190 --> 00:16:48.960
and more batch stuff.

00:16:48.960 --> 00:16:51.800
So check those out
for more details.

00:16:51.800 --> 00:16:53.770
And we'll move up one
layer of the stack.

00:16:53.770 --> 00:16:57.030
So now we're going to move
to Container Engine, which

00:16:57.030 --> 00:16:59.100
is hosted Kubernetes,
which for running

00:16:59.100 --> 00:17:00.960
containerized applications.

00:17:00.960 --> 00:17:03.780
So how many people here
are using containers now

00:17:03.780 --> 00:17:04.500
in some form?

00:17:04.500 --> 00:17:07.829
OK, big chunk, like, what,
75%, 80% of the room.

00:17:07.829 --> 00:17:10.680
And then I'm just going to
assume everyone's interested,

00:17:10.680 --> 00:17:12.560
because this is my talk.

00:17:15.569 --> 00:17:18.300
So containers are exciting
for a bunch of reasons.

00:17:18.300 --> 00:17:20.730
And to just highlight a
few things very quickly,

00:17:20.730 --> 00:17:21.960
you get isolation.

00:17:21.960 --> 00:17:23.069
You get repeatability.

00:17:23.069 --> 00:17:26.069
You can bring your same work
into different environments,

00:17:26.069 --> 00:17:27.712
and have it behave the same.

00:17:27.712 --> 00:17:29.670
As a developer, I think
of it like solving the,

00:17:29.670 --> 00:17:33.480
worked on my machine, problem.

00:17:33.480 --> 00:17:36.720
And that consistency
is really valuable.

00:17:36.720 --> 00:17:39.530
You end up wanting
to use a lot of them.

00:17:39.530 --> 00:17:41.810
And you want to use them
on a lot of machines.

00:17:41.810 --> 00:17:43.610
And that's where
Kubernetes comes in.

00:17:43.610 --> 00:17:46.130
And Kubernetes is an
open source project

00:17:46.130 --> 00:17:48.890
that was started by
Google, and very quickly,

00:17:48.890 --> 00:17:50.480
moved into the open.

00:17:50.480 --> 00:17:53.210
This has been built by a
community of people, including

00:17:53.210 --> 00:17:56.870
Google, and many, many other
companies and individuals.

00:17:56.870 --> 00:18:00.110
It is inspired by some
patterns that we know work

00:18:00.110 --> 00:18:03.230
on running lots of
containers at scale,

00:18:03.230 --> 00:18:05.630
but informed by that
community of people

00:18:05.630 --> 00:18:07.940
who are trying to use it in
places that aren't Google.

00:18:07.940 --> 00:18:11.390
Sometimes, have our
own look at things.

00:18:11.390 --> 00:18:14.630
So that just is a
fantastic place to be.

00:18:14.630 --> 00:18:16.580
It's already very mature.

00:18:16.580 --> 00:18:19.070
It can scale up to you,
I think, with the 1.6

00:18:19.070 --> 00:18:25.010
they're hitting the SLAs
at 5,000 node clusters.

00:18:25.010 --> 00:18:27.940
You may have seen the Pokemon
Go news, that sort of thing.

00:18:27.940 --> 00:18:29.344
That's all on Kubernetes.

00:18:32.070 --> 00:18:34.700
And it really lets you think
about applications, not

00:18:34.700 --> 00:18:37.310
specific computers.

00:18:37.310 --> 00:18:40.460
And the mental model I
like to use for this is

00:18:40.460 --> 00:18:43.040
think about it as like
a white board, right?

00:18:43.040 --> 00:18:44.570
When you talk to
your colleagues,

00:18:44.570 --> 00:18:49.130
you're usually drawing lines
and boxes of how the system--

00:18:49.130 --> 00:18:51.950
what pieces there are, how
they talk to each other,

00:18:51.950 --> 00:18:54.590
and what the data
flows look like.

00:18:54.590 --> 00:18:56.570
That's how you talk to
Kubernetes, as well.

00:18:56.570 --> 00:18:58.010
So when you
configure Kubernetes,

00:18:58.010 --> 00:19:01.130
you say, I want to
have 10 web front ends.

00:19:01.130 --> 00:19:02.530
And they should be named Foo.

00:19:02.530 --> 00:19:05.570
And I want to have these
five back end processors.

00:19:05.570 --> 00:19:08.390
And they should be able to
find the database at this name.

00:19:08.390 --> 00:19:11.270
And you can even
have kind of proxies

00:19:11.270 --> 00:19:13.820
for things that are outside of
your cluster that are named.

00:19:13.820 --> 00:19:15.736
And they can all find
each other the same way.

00:19:15.736 --> 00:19:18.410
So you basically, when you're
thinking about your systems

00:19:18.410 --> 00:19:19.910
in Kubernetes,
you're thinking about

00:19:19.910 --> 00:19:22.340
the logical infrastructure,
how the pieces connect

00:19:22.340 --> 00:19:24.320
to each other.

00:19:24.320 --> 00:19:26.160
And that is really powerful.

00:19:26.160 --> 00:19:27.410
So what are the programs?

00:19:27.410 --> 00:19:28.368
How are they connected?

00:19:28.368 --> 00:19:31.070
Where do they store
their information?

00:19:31.070 --> 00:19:33.782
Container Engine is
hosted Kubernetes.

00:19:33.782 --> 00:19:35.240
So there's two
parts to Kubernetes.

00:19:35.240 --> 00:19:37.250
You've got to have a cluster,
and have it up and running.

00:19:37.250 --> 00:19:38.790
And then you use the cluster.

00:19:38.790 --> 00:19:41.129
And Container Engine is
focused on just solving

00:19:41.129 --> 00:19:42.920
this problem for you
of keeping the cluster

00:19:42.920 --> 00:19:46.640
running, and updated, and happy,
changing its size as your load

00:19:46.640 --> 00:19:49.440
changes, that sort of thing.

00:19:49.440 --> 00:19:52.250
So there are a few
constraints here.

00:19:52.250 --> 00:19:54.030
It is a container first system.

00:19:54.030 --> 00:19:58.010
So you need to have your
software in containers.

00:19:58.010 --> 00:20:00.020
And there are some
architectural constraints.

00:20:00.020 --> 00:20:04.370
Like, it's designed for
most of the processes

00:20:04.370 --> 00:20:05.870
to come and go over time.

00:20:05.870 --> 00:20:09.200
There's some tools in new
releases for handling things

00:20:09.200 --> 00:20:11.360
that need very specific
state, that need to a disk

00:20:11.360 --> 00:20:13.970
available to them
wherever they move.

00:20:13.970 --> 00:20:16.034
But if you have an
existing system,

00:20:16.034 --> 00:20:18.200
you're probably going to
need to take a bit of time,

00:20:18.200 --> 00:20:19.616
and think about
your architecture,

00:20:19.616 --> 00:20:22.770
and how it maps with containers,
and this sort of thing.

00:20:22.770 --> 00:20:25.130
It's a really good
fit for systems

00:20:25.130 --> 00:20:27.300
that need to run in lots
of different environments.

00:20:27.300 --> 00:20:29.000
So you can think at
the logical level,

00:20:29.000 --> 00:20:31.730
and then deploy this using
the same configuration

00:20:31.730 --> 00:20:34.310
on different clouds,
on your own machines,

00:20:34.310 --> 00:20:38.480
in dev test prod environments,
all of these different things.

00:20:38.480 --> 00:20:41.270
And it gives you a
lot of portability

00:20:41.270 --> 00:20:44.180
of entire workloads,
whole systems.

00:20:44.180 --> 00:20:45.170
That's really powerful.

00:20:45.170 --> 00:20:48.247
It's designed for
containers up front.

00:20:48.247 --> 00:20:49.580
And I will say, it's a good fit.

00:20:49.580 --> 00:20:52.070
But this is also a
little bit constraint-y.

00:20:52.070 --> 00:20:54.260
You need to have
good communication

00:20:54.260 --> 00:20:56.330
across your organization
with your teams.

00:20:56.330 --> 00:21:00.277
Because these containers are
the kind of unit of deployment.

00:21:00.277 --> 00:21:02.360
This is the process, and
all of its configuration,

00:21:02.360 --> 00:21:03.151
that sort of thing.

00:21:03.151 --> 00:21:06.740
So your dev team, and your ops
team, and your security teams,

00:21:06.740 --> 00:21:09.904
they actually all need to be
able to make changes to these.

00:21:09.904 --> 00:21:11.820
And so hopefully, there's
a build environment.

00:21:11.820 --> 00:21:14.070
And hopefully, they
have good communication.

00:21:14.070 --> 00:21:16.880
So that's a place where
it's a good fit, which

00:21:16.880 --> 00:21:20.030
implies you probably want
a good build environment,

00:21:20.030 --> 00:21:22.250
some sort of
continuous integration,

00:21:22.250 --> 00:21:26.060
continuous deployment,
perhaps, that sort of thing.

00:21:26.060 --> 00:21:29.220
And it works with layer
3 networks and above,

00:21:29.220 --> 00:21:32.530
so you can host whatever
you want if the pieces talk

00:21:32.530 --> 00:21:34.320
to each other.

00:21:34.320 --> 00:21:37.880
So a quick demo on this note.

00:21:37.880 --> 00:21:44.530
So now we're in the Container
Engine portion of the web UI.

00:21:44.530 --> 00:21:47.000
And in here, I've actually
already created cluster,

00:21:47.000 --> 00:21:48.980
as you do on cooking
shows and demos.

00:21:53.330 --> 00:21:56.727
It takes about five minutes
to start of cluster, usually.

00:21:56.727 --> 00:21:59.060
And this cluster, there's a
bunch of settings and things

00:21:59.060 --> 00:21:59.780
we can poke around at.

00:21:59.780 --> 00:22:00.770
But one thing I
wanted to highlight

00:22:00.770 --> 00:22:02.840
is this is just using
a managed instance

00:22:02.840 --> 00:22:04.010
group from Compute Engine.

00:22:04.010 --> 00:22:05.930
And these are the
VMs under the covers.

00:22:05.930 --> 00:22:09.500
So Kubernetes is actually
hitting the same API

00:22:09.500 --> 00:22:11.120
as a Compute Engine
that you could

00:22:11.120 --> 00:22:14.240
hit if you want to do your
own orchestration stuff.

00:22:14.240 --> 00:22:16.640
And the auto scaling works
via that, and the like.

00:22:16.640 --> 00:22:21.016
So this is the open source
dashboard for Kubernetes.

00:22:21.016 --> 00:22:22.640
And we can see that
it's actually got--

00:22:22.640 --> 00:22:25.460
the same nodes are listed there.

00:22:25.460 --> 00:22:28.580
I've gone ahead and created a
thing called a service, which

00:22:28.580 --> 00:22:30.380
is just basically
a load balancer

00:22:30.380 --> 00:22:32.930
for the cluster for
this particular app

00:22:32.930 --> 00:22:35.220
that I'm going to deploy.

00:22:35.220 --> 00:22:36.770
And it's called nginx.

00:22:36.770 --> 00:22:39.260
And it's on a public
IP address if anybody

00:22:39.260 --> 00:22:40.280
can type really quickly.

00:22:40.280 --> 00:22:41.900
But there's nothing there.

00:22:41.900 --> 00:22:46.820
It doesn't go, because
there's no software running.

00:22:46.820 --> 00:22:48.650
We can see that over here.

00:22:48.650 --> 00:22:50.720
So let's close that up.

00:22:50.720 --> 00:22:55.040
And there's a command line
tool called cube control

00:22:55.040 --> 00:22:58.790
that talks to its REST APIs.

00:22:58.790 --> 00:23:00.715
And we can run.

00:23:00.715 --> 00:23:02.090
There's a little
bit of shorthand

00:23:02.090 --> 00:23:03.506
if you just want
to run one image.

00:23:03.506 --> 00:23:04.550
So I'm going to use that.

00:23:04.550 --> 00:23:07.610
I'm going to call
it nginx, which is--

00:23:07.610 --> 00:23:09.800
you may of may not have
heard of-- it's a very, very

00:23:09.800 --> 00:23:12.110
popular web server.

00:23:12.110 --> 00:23:18.720
And it's based on an image
that is called nginx.

00:23:18.720 --> 00:23:25.410
And this is a Docker image
curated by the nginx folks

00:23:25.410 --> 00:23:27.959
that we're pointing out here.

00:23:27.959 --> 00:23:29.000
OK, what did it do there?

00:23:29.000 --> 00:23:29.583
That was fast.

00:23:29.583 --> 00:23:31.890
Thw And it's already up.

00:23:31.890 --> 00:23:33.010
This is deployed.

00:23:33.010 --> 00:23:36.330
And if we come back over here
to services, click on this,

00:23:36.330 --> 00:23:38.970
we get the happy
default nginx screen.

00:23:41.590 --> 00:23:45.520
So that's kind of awesome.

00:23:45.520 --> 00:23:50.420
Just for fun-- so we have one
of these running right now.

00:23:50.420 --> 00:23:52.840
So there's a bunch of
things that happened here.

00:23:52.840 --> 00:23:55.600
And I'm going to refer you to an
introduction to Kubernetes talk

00:23:55.600 --> 00:23:58.320
to go through what
these concepts all are.

00:23:58.320 --> 00:24:00.320
But basically, I said, I
want to run this image.

00:24:00.320 --> 00:24:02.350
And it kind of patched
it up in the management

00:24:02.350 --> 00:24:04.090
abstractions that
Kubernetes uses.

00:24:04.090 --> 00:24:07.110
So it puts the
containers in a pod.

00:24:07.110 --> 00:24:08.660
And that's what gets scheduled.

00:24:08.660 --> 00:24:11.350
And then we have these replica
sets, which are the templates,

00:24:11.350 --> 00:24:13.216
and how many of the
pods should be running.

00:24:13.216 --> 00:24:15.340
And a deployment lets you
roll forward and backward

00:24:15.340 --> 00:24:17.680
among these things.

00:24:17.680 --> 00:24:19.880
So we have, by default,
just one of one.

00:24:19.880 --> 00:24:21.880
And so I can come back
over here, and I can say,

00:24:21.880 --> 00:24:28.820
can you control a
scale deployment nginx.

00:24:28.820 --> 00:24:35.390
And I want to have,
let's say, 12 replicas.

00:24:35.390 --> 00:24:37.926
And it says, OK, scaled, baby.

00:24:37.926 --> 00:24:39.050
And we come back over here.

00:24:39.050 --> 00:24:40.820
And 11 of 12 of
those are running.

00:24:40.820 --> 00:24:43.220
And refresh again, and
12 of 12 are running.

00:24:43.220 --> 00:24:44.970
And one of those are
running for a minute.

00:24:44.970 --> 00:24:46.261
The other ones are six seconds.

00:24:46.261 --> 00:24:51.170
So you can very quickly respond
to new needs in your system.

00:24:51.170 --> 00:24:53.750
And this is very, very high
level, but just kind of

00:24:53.750 --> 00:24:59.170
to get a feel of what it might
be like to use Kubernetes.

00:24:59.170 --> 00:25:02.050
And we have great talks.

00:25:02.050 --> 00:25:06.490
So today, right for lunch, from
Kelsey Hightower, I believe,

00:25:06.490 --> 00:25:10.377
is a nice introduction
to containers.

00:25:10.377 --> 00:25:10.960
Oh, no, sorry.

00:25:10.960 --> 00:25:11.876
That's the second one.

00:25:11.876 --> 00:25:13.760
So 2:40 is Kelsey's.

00:25:13.760 --> 00:25:16.905
There's an introduction
to Kubernetes at 1:00--

00:25:16.905 --> 00:25:19.030
or whatever the times have
turned into after lunch.

00:25:19.030 --> 00:25:19.870
It might be that.

00:25:19.870 --> 00:25:22.210
Or it might be a little later.

00:25:22.210 --> 00:25:25.120
And so check these out,
either today in person,

00:25:25.120 --> 00:25:28.510
or online later.

00:25:28.510 --> 00:25:31.720
And now we move up one
layer to App Engine.

00:25:31.720 --> 00:25:34.330
And now we're
talking code first.

00:25:34.330 --> 00:25:36.460
The abstraction
that you use when

00:25:36.460 --> 00:25:40.120
you're talking to App Engine
is like, I have my source code.

00:25:40.120 --> 00:25:43.120
And the idea with App Engine,
the goal from the beginning,

00:25:43.120 --> 00:25:45.850
is let developers
focus on their code,

00:25:45.850 --> 00:25:49.244
and have the infrastructure
handle the rest.

00:25:49.244 --> 00:25:51.160
A Little bit snarky,
we'll say it's serverless

00:25:51.160 --> 00:25:53.500
before it was cool.

00:25:53.500 --> 00:25:56.590
But it started in 2008
with this trade off.

00:25:56.590 --> 00:25:59.800
If you write it this way,
then we'll scale it for you.

00:25:59.800 --> 00:26:01.570
And there were some
constraints about how

00:26:01.570 --> 00:26:03.236
you had to shape your
applications, what

00:26:03.236 --> 00:26:06.700
APIs you could use to get
that automatic scaling.

00:26:06.700 --> 00:26:09.760
We have a new flexible
runtime environment

00:26:09.760 --> 00:26:13.090
that is very much informed by
the modern container world.

00:26:13.090 --> 00:26:15.550
So it is Docker containers
under the covers.

00:26:15.550 --> 00:26:18.040
And therefore, you can
run any application that

00:26:18.040 --> 00:26:20.260
will run in a Docker container.

00:26:20.260 --> 00:26:21.370
It is still web-focused.

00:26:21.370 --> 00:26:23.260
So it's primarily
geared towards things

00:26:23.260 --> 00:26:27.490
that are HTTP requests
responses working through.

00:26:27.490 --> 00:26:31.990
We have run times that we
maintain for these languages

00:26:31.990 --> 00:26:33.160
listed here.

00:26:33.160 --> 00:26:35.110
But also, if you
can run something

00:26:35.110 --> 00:26:37.660
that listens to HTTP be
in a Docker container,

00:26:37.660 --> 00:26:39.850
you can deploy that in
whatever language you have,

00:26:39.850 --> 00:26:41.559
whatever tools
you use currently.

00:26:41.559 --> 00:26:43.850
Or something you've got set
up for another environment,

00:26:43.850 --> 00:26:46.310
you can use it here.

00:26:46.310 --> 00:26:48.010
So that's the high level thing.

00:26:48.010 --> 00:26:51.040
You, when you're working with
it, you think about code.

00:26:51.040 --> 00:26:53.620
You think about the kind
of HTTP requests responses.

00:26:53.620 --> 00:26:54.774
And then you might--

00:26:54.774 --> 00:26:55.690
this isn't quite core.

00:26:55.690 --> 00:26:57.430
But you think about
like, which version

00:26:57.430 --> 00:26:58.846
is served in
production right now,

00:26:58.846 --> 00:27:01.990
and like, roll forward and
back, and that sort of thing.

00:27:01.990 --> 00:27:04.390
Constraints-wise,
the kind of standard

00:27:04.390 --> 00:27:06.310
run times that we've
had forever and we're

00:27:06.310 --> 00:27:08.800
going to keep working
with, we just updated Java,

00:27:08.800 --> 00:27:10.420
continuing there.

00:27:10.420 --> 00:27:14.260
But we have run times for
Python, Java, PHP, and Go.

00:27:14.260 --> 00:27:16.400
These are a very
constrained, fixed run times.

00:27:16.400 --> 00:27:20.410
So you can't add new
binaries to it, for example.

00:27:20.410 --> 00:27:25.300
In return, you get this very
fast performance out of it.

00:27:25.300 --> 00:27:27.820
The flexible run times, they
have whatever constraints

00:27:27.820 --> 00:27:29.320
are inherent in Docker.

00:27:29.320 --> 00:27:33.980
It's kind of like HTTP
focused in this context.

00:27:33.980 --> 00:27:36.400
And the way they're
implemented right today

00:27:36.400 --> 00:27:40.060
might not be the best fit
for really low traffic sites.

00:27:40.060 --> 00:27:42.505
We keep two VMs running
under the covers,

00:27:42.505 --> 00:27:44.380
so that there's always
something going there.

00:27:44.380 --> 00:27:46.129
So if you have
really low traffic,

00:27:46.129 --> 00:27:47.420
your end up paying for two VMs.

00:27:47.420 --> 00:27:50.234
And that's not perfect
in that scenario.

00:27:50.234 --> 00:27:51.400
There's work going on there.

00:27:51.400 --> 00:27:55.330
But where things stand
today, there's that.

00:27:55.330 --> 00:27:58.120
All of App Engine is a good
fit for apps that are primarily

00:27:58.120 --> 00:28:01.060
HTTP request response,
that are web-focused,

00:28:01.060 --> 00:28:03.370
that are developer heavy.

00:28:03.370 --> 00:28:04.900
You may scale to high traffic.

00:28:04.900 --> 00:28:09.160
You may seen the
snapshot kind of things.

00:28:09.160 --> 00:28:11.360
Santa Tracker from Google
is run an App Engine,

00:28:11.360 --> 00:28:13.480
whole bunch of stuff.

00:28:13.480 --> 00:28:20.650
I think we're doing 100 billion
App Engine requests per day.

00:28:20.650 --> 00:28:24.040
There's a lot of applications
running on App Engine.

00:28:24.040 --> 00:28:28.090
So what that might look
like in practice, I

00:28:28.090 --> 00:28:31.590
have another app deployed,
as you might guess.

00:28:31.590 --> 00:28:34.090
This is the flexible run times.

00:28:34.090 --> 00:28:36.440
It is an app to show
you pictures of fruit.

00:28:36.440 --> 00:28:43.209
This is a very highly demanded
application, so I had write it.

00:28:43.209 --> 00:28:45.750
We can see, at a high level,
you have these kind of services.

00:28:45.750 --> 00:28:48.035
So that's one portion
of your application.

00:28:48.035 --> 00:28:49.660
In this case, it's
the default version.

00:28:49.660 --> 00:28:51.454
So it's at root.

00:28:51.454 --> 00:28:52.870
And if I click on
this link, it'll

00:28:52.870 --> 00:28:55.090
take me to the
photo of the thing.

00:28:55.090 --> 00:28:56.170
And we have some lemons.

00:28:56.170 --> 00:28:59.230
And I can refresh
that over and over.

00:28:59.230 --> 00:29:01.630
And I will get lemons all day.

00:29:01.630 --> 00:29:04.540
And you know, maybe
we don't want lemons.

00:29:04.540 --> 00:29:06.880
I got some feedback that
maybe life and lemons is not

00:29:06.880 --> 00:29:08.320
the best thing.

00:29:08.320 --> 00:29:11.290
So we've deployed
some other versions.

00:29:11.290 --> 00:29:13.630
So we can go for cherry.

00:29:13.630 --> 00:29:15.820
And the other versions,
they're currently

00:29:15.820 --> 00:29:18.280
not serving any
traffic publicly.

00:29:18.280 --> 00:29:21.880
But we can still go for test,
or verification, or whatever.

00:29:21.880 --> 00:29:25.260
We can go to a sub-domain and
hit that version directly,

00:29:25.260 --> 00:29:26.210
and see that there.

00:29:26.210 --> 00:29:28.900
Oh, we get cherries
on this version.

00:29:28.900 --> 00:29:30.010
So we can do apples.

00:29:30.010 --> 00:29:34.870
Or probably, durian is
where we want to be, right?

00:29:34.870 --> 00:29:37.326
How many feel like durian?

00:29:37.326 --> 00:29:38.950
Oh, we got one.

00:29:38.950 --> 00:29:43.360
OK, maybe durian isn't the
most popular in this room.

00:29:43.360 --> 00:29:45.280
But we've gotten
requests for durian.

00:29:45.280 --> 00:29:50.380
And what we're going
to do is migrate

00:29:50.380 --> 00:29:54.280
all of the public traffic
to this version of the app.

00:29:54.280 --> 00:29:56.590
And then we come back over here.

00:29:56.590 --> 00:29:59.350
I'll click here to
make sure it's real.

00:29:59.350 --> 00:30:03.021
That version is actually
serving all of our traffic.

00:30:03.021 --> 00:30:05.020
So then we've had the
public moved over to that.

00:30:05.020 --> 00:30:07.478
If we needed to roll back, we
can just do the version back,

00:30:07.478 --> 00:30:08.140
as well.

00:30:08.140 --> 00:30:11.050
We can do more complex
traffic splitting.

00:30:11.050 --> 00:30:16.296
We can say we want
to give, actually,

00:30:16.296 --> 00:30:17.710
lemon most of the traffic.

00:30:17.710 --> 00:30:20.590
But we want to
actually give durian

00:30:20.590 --> 00:30:24.610
to 15% of the people who
come, something like that.

00:30:24.610 --> 00:30:27.960
So you can do that sort of
thing as well at a high level.

00:30:27.960 --> 00:30:30.110
And of course, you
get tons of stats.

00:30:30.110 --> 00:30:32.230
This app, as you
can imagine, isn't

00:30:32.230 --> 00:30:34.180
getting a whole lot of traffic.

00:30:34.180 --> 00:30:38.440
But you'll get nice stats
for how much traffic

00:30:38.440 --> 00:30:40.600
you're getting,
latency, all sorts

00:30:40.600 --> 00:30:44.320
of different metrics about
your application, full logs,

00:30:44.320 --> 00:30:46.429
and that sort of thing.

00:30:46.429 --> 00:30:47.720
So that's what that looks like.

00:30:47.720 --> 00:30:51.670
So why might you
choose App Engine?

00:30:51.670 --> 00:30:55.810
To focus on your code, and
have that scale, and go.

00:30:55.810 --> 00:30:57.910
It is very much geared
towards web workloads.

00:30:57.910 --> 00:31:01.270
If that's where you are, it's
going to be like a good fit.

00:31:01.270 --> 00:31:04.540
The new flexible run
times are very portable.

00:31:04.540 --> 00:31:06.280
So you can take these
Docker containers

00:31:06.280 --> 00:31:08.738
that are produced by this system
and run them other places.

00:31:08.738 --> 00:31:11.320
Or you can take container images
that you've preset elsewhere

00:31:11.320 --> 00:31:14.020
and run them in flex.

00:31:14.020 --> 00:31:16.000
It is a fully
managed environment.

00:31:16.000 --> 00:31:18.214
But it is also running on
the same infrastructure.

00:31:18.214 --> 00:31:19.630
So from the flexible
environments,

00:31:19.630 --> 00:31:22.780
you can actually, what we
call, kind of break glass.

00:31:22.780 --> 00:31:24.730
You can go in SSH
into the machine,

00:31:24.730 --> 00:31:27.310
and use your regular
debugging tools

00:31:27.310 --> 00:31:30.190
to investigate some
situation that's come up.

00:31:30.190 --> 00:31:32.890
And then you just remove
that VM from rotation.

00:31:32.890 --> 00:31:33.849
And it makes a new one.

00:31:33.849 --> 00:31:35.848
And things are back the
way they were before you

00:31:35.848 --> 00:31:36.820
started poking around.

00:31:36.820 --> 00:31:38.278
But you've been
able to investigate

00:31:38.278 --> 00:31:42.160
using the tools you're
familiar with and know well,

00:31:42.160 --> 00:31:44.180
and a hundred billion
requests a day.

00:31:44.180 --> 00:31:47.350
So a lot more talks
on this today,

00:31:47.350 --> 00:31:54.790
including .NET running .NET
containers on Google Cloud.

00:31:54.790 --> 00:31:58.660
Scaling things, a talking
geared towards the Ruby world,

00:31:58.660 --> 00:32:01.397
and how this works really,
really well on Flex.

00:32:01.397 --> 00:32:03.480
If you were coming from
Python or other languages,

00:32:03.480 --> 00:32:06.760
actually, that talk will
also be pretty relevant,

00:32:06.760 --> 00:32:09.730
how the budgeting works,
and that sort of thing.

00:32:09.730 --> 00:32:12.172
And the demo that
I barely skimmed

00:32:12.172 --> 00:32:14.130
the top of, if you want
to see more about that,

00:32:14.130 --> 00:32:17.170
the talk list at the
bottom, I swiped that demo

00:32:17.170 --> 00:32:18.564
from Justin Beckwith.

00:32:18.564 --> 00:32:20.230
So you could see the
full version there.

00:32:20.230 --> 00:32:22.250
And he'll go into more detail.

00:32:22.250 --> 00:32:27.850
OK, up one more level in
the stack, cloud functions.

00:32:27.850 --> 00:32:30.647
So this is just--

00:32:30.647 --> 00:32:32.980
if you could imagine just
having a function in the cloud

00:32:32.980 --> 00:32:36.010
that does work for you.

00:32:36.010 --> 00:32:39.080
There's so many different
APIs on the internet.

00:32:39.080 --> 00:32:41.950
Most of them are
available over HTTP.

00:32:41.950 --> 00:32:44.950
And we start needing to glue
them together, and figure out

00:32:44.950 --> 00:32:48.640
how to connect them, and either
augment our existing systems,

00:32:48.640 --> 00:32:50.590
or in some cases,
actually build systems

00:32:50.590 --> 00:32:53.104
out of connecting
the pieces together.

00:32:53.104 --> 00:32:54.520
And I'm particularly
excited here.

00:32:54.520 --> 00:32:58.540
Because I think this can
allow people to kind of go

00:32:58.540 --> 00:33:01.660
a step beyond what we
normally think about

00:33:01.660 --> 00:33:03.570
as our current
primary developer IT

00:33:03.570 --> 00:33:05.680
ops audience, people
inside of organizations,

00:33:05.680 --> 00:33:08.770
automating things, gluing
things together, doing data

00:33:08.770 --> 00:33:11.590
transformations, and
things like that.

00:33:11.590 --> 00:33:14.980
It is primarily event driven,
so either like an HTTP request

00:33:14.980 --> 00:33:18.910
comes in, or a new object
is added to a Cloud Storage

00:33:18.910 --> 00:33:21.310
bucket, and a
notification happens.

00:33:21.310 --> 00:33:24.310
Or we have this thing
called Cloud Pub/Sub,

00:33:24.310 --> 00:33:28.300
which is a message
bus-like thing in cloud.

00:33:28.300 --> 00:33:29.830
Did you know we
have this thing that

00:33:29.830 --> 00:33:32.290
is both high throughput
and durable for all

00:33:32.290 --> 00:33:33.639
of your messages?

00:33:33.639 --> 00:33:34.180
Check it out.

00:33:34.180 --> 00:33:36.500
It's awesome.

00:33:36.500 --> 00:33:38.950
And for my biases to
show, basically, I

00:33:38.950 --> 00:33:41.410
think Pub/Sub and
Cloud Storage are

00:33:41.410 --> 00:33:44.020
APIs that nearly every
op should investigate.

00:33:44.020 --> 00:33:46.120
Check them out.

00:33:46.120 --> 00:33:48.910
It is very much serverless.

00:33:48.910 --> 00:33:51.610
I put it in quotes
because there's computers

00:33:51.610 --> 00:33:53.462
down underneath.

00:33:53.462 --> 00:33:55.420
But from your perspective,
you have a function.

00:33:55.420 --> 00:33:56.752
You write the code.

00:33:56.752 --> 00:33:57.460
You push that up.

00:33:57.460 --> 00:33:58.490
And it gets run.

00:33:58.490 --> 00:33:59.920
Pay only for what you use.

00:33:59.920 --> 00:34:01.990
The run time is
currently Node.js.

00:34:01.990 --> 00:34:04.480
So you can take
these same functions

00:34:04.480 --> 00:34:07.000
and run them in other
environments, as well.

00:34:07.000 --> 00:34:09.062
So if you want to run
the same implementation,

00:34:09.062 --> 00:34:10.520
you can run it on
your own Machine.

00:34:10.520 --> 00:34:12.010
You could run other places.

00:34:12.010 --> 00:34:15.670
So as the user of these--
usually the developer--

00:34:15.670 --> 00:34:18.250
you think about events,
and you think about,

00:34:18.250 --> 00:34:20.560
so what happens in
the system, and then

00:34:20.560 --> 00:34:23.980
what you want to do to transform
that, what your function does.

00:34:23.980 --> 00:34:27.050
And that's almost it.

00:34:27.050 --> 00:34:31.489
Constraints, today it's
JavaScript on node.

00:34:31.489 --> 00:34:34.590
So that's the language we get.

00:34:34.590 --> 00:34:38.590
You are thinking about
things at the function level.

00:34:38.590 --> 00:34:41.260
So I think of this
more in terms of like,

00:34:41.260 --> 00:34:43.719
as your system gets
larger and larger,

00:34:43.719 --> 00:34:46.150
there are some cognitive
overlap-- you know, [INAUDIBLE]

00:34:46.150 --> 00:34:48.909
to kind of think about.

00:34:48.909 --> 00:34:51.790
And the system must
work through events.

00:34:51.790 --> 00:34:55.454
It is almost pure
surrealist promise.

00:34:55.454 --> 00:34:56.620
You just take this function.

00:34:56.620 --> 00:34:57.536
And we run it for you.

00:34:57.536 --> 00:34:59.080
And they scale incredibly high.

00:34:59.080 --> 00:35:02.230
We've got people doing IOT
workload handling, where

00:35:02.230 --> 00:35:04.240
they have devices in
hundreds of thousands

00:35:04.240 --> 00:35:08.200
of different locations
all pushing their data up.

00:35:08.200 --> 00:35:10.960
And the same function
definitions scales up and down,

00:35:10.960 --> 00:35:13.900
depending upon the load there.

00:35:13.900 --> 00:35:15.730
If you are using
Pub/Sub and Storage,

00:35:15.730 --> 00:35:18.350
it's a really, really good fit.

00:35:18.350 --> 00:35:21.670
If you've got some kind of
somewhat lightweight ETL data

00:35:21.670 --> 00:35:23.572
transformations, if
you've got some JSON that

00:35:23.572 --> 00:35:25.030
comes in some
format, and you tweak

00:35:25.030 --> 00:35:28.480
it a bit for some other
system, super good fit.

00:35:28.480 --> 00:35:30.560
Or just in general, I
like to think it like,

00:35:30.560 --> 00:35:34.810
it's the glue for all of
these Cloud HTTP Functions.

00:35:34.810 --> 00:35:36.640
So is internet
duct tape or glue.

00:35:36.640 --> 00:35:39.580
Put all the things together.

00:35:39.580 --> 00:35:44.260
Demo here is more of
kind of like a tour.

00:35:44.260 --> 00:35:48.670
This is the Cloud Functions
portion of the web UI.

00:35:48.670 --> 00:35:54.010
Just to kind of pick on
HelloWorld, as one might do,

00:35:54.010 --> 00:35:55.840
we again, get a bunch
of stats, information

00:35:55.840 --> 00:35:59.820
about how this thing's working.

00:35:59.820 --> 00:36:02.250
The trigger for this
is an HTTP endpoint.

00:36:02.250 --> 00:36:07.080
So you can do a get or
post request against this.

00:36:07.080 --> 00:36:12.109
If I do that, I
get some text back.

00:36:12.109 --> 00:36:12.900
No message to find.

00:36:12.900 --> 00:36:14.280
Where did that come from?

00:36:14.280 --> 00:36:16.990
We can also see the source code.

00:36:16.990 --> 00:36:18.930
This is a very,
very simple function

00:36:18.930 --> 00:36:22.980
that is looking for
JSON Dock to come in,

00:36:22.980 --> 00:36:28.161
and to see if it has a message
key, and then do something.

00:36:28.161 --> 00:36:30.660
If that doesn't happen, we get
the message that we just saw.

00:36:30.660 --> 00:36:33.660
So let's try to do
something closer

00:36:33.660 --> 00:36:37.200
to what the function expects.

00:36:37.200 --> 00:36:39.300
So I'll go ahead
and paste that in.

00:36:45.700 --> 00:36:46.370
Do that.

00:36:46.370 --> 00:36:48.980
And we test the function.

00:36:48.980 --> 00:36:52.520
And we get, basically,
log output, in this case.

00:36:52.520 --> 00:36:55.070
But it gives you a sense
of both how quickly

00:36:55.070 --> 00:36:58.490
the functions execute, a
little bit of play environment.

00:36:58.490 --> 00:37:01.500
And you can connect almost
anything together this way.

00:37:01.500 --> 00:37:04.190
And this environment
will also let you see

00:37:04.190 --> 00:37:06.370
the actual console log output.

00:37:06.370 --> 00:37:10.290
So you can see what came out
of your logging as you go--

00:37:10.290 --> 00:37:13.680
so just a quick
feel of Functions.

00:37:13.680 --> 00:37:15.055
So with Functions,
you don't even

00:37:15.055 --> 00:37:16.513
have to think about
servers at all.

00:37:16.513 --> 00:37:18.680
You purely have the
event that comes in,

00:37:18.680 --> 00:37:21.560
what you do with it, move on.

00:37:21.560 --> 00:37:23.330
You pay only for what you use.

00:37:23.330 --> 00:37:27.410
Don't need to think about
the big architecture pieces.

00:37:27.410 --> 00:37:29.570
And it is very
straightforward for developers

00:37:29.570 --> 00:37:33.620
to reason about in the local.

00:37:33.620 --> 00:37:35.120
You just have your inputs.

00:37:35.120 --> 00:37:37.730
And then you have some
side effects, some outputs.

00:37:37.730 --> 00:37:39.890
And you go.

00:37:39.890 --> 00:37:42.015
As everbody knows, once
you have a lot of those,

00:37:42.015 --> 00:37:42.890
there's complexities.

00:37:42.890 --> 00:37:46.010
But the model, while you're
working on Functions,

00:37:46.010 --> 00:37:48.290
is very, very clean.

00:37:48.290 --> 00:37:50.150
To learn more about
this, we have a talk

00:37:50.150 --> 00:37:52.057
specifically on
serverless architectures,

00:37:52.057 --> 00:37:54.265
and how to think about them,
and where they run well.

00:37:54.265 --> 00:37:56.840
It's mostly around Functions.

00:37:56.840 --> 00:37:59.900
Later today-- or two of them,
actually-- today and tomorrow.

00:37:59.900 --> 00:38:01.400
The second one is
actually where I--

00:38:03.877 --> 00:38:04.460
head this way.

00:38:04.460 --> 00:38:06.126
And actually, that's
not the demo there.

00:38:06.126 --> 00:38:08.510
So anyway, apologies.

00:38:08.510 --> 00:38:10.340
And now we get to this kind of--

00:38:10.340 --> 00:38:12.820
so we've done the run through.

00:38:12.820 --> 00:38:15.245
Did we find a good
fit for folks as we

00:38:15.245 --> 00:38:18.250
were walking through this?

00:38:18.250 --> 00:38:21.290
I think, for some folks here,
one or two of those things

00:38:21.290 --> 00:38:24.080
jumped out as, OK, that's the
thing I should look into next.

00:38:24.080 --> 00:38:26.370
For other folks,
it's not so clear.

00:38:26.370 --> 00:38:28.820
I'm still kind of confused.

00:38:28.820 --> 00:38:31.300
Or maybe I want to use multiple.

00:38:31.300 --> 00:38:32.840
And it does depend.

00:38:32.840 --> 00:38:37.030
So here's a few more directions
to think about this from.

00:38:37.030 --> 00:38:40.910
So the abstraction level is kind
of the structure of the talk.

00:38:40.910 --> 00:38:45.860
We're looking at different
ways to think about an offer

00:38:45.860 --> 00:38:47.420
rate on your code.

00:38:47.420 --> 00:38:49.757
Also, there are just sort
of technical requirements.

00:38:49.757 --> 00:38:51.590
So you might need certain
network protocols.

00:38:51.590 --> 00:38:53.420
You might need
certain other things.

00:38:53.420 --> 00:38:55.580
That might change your
decision, and just

00:38:55.580 --> 00:38:58.550
push you directly into one
answer, or a subset of answers.

00:38:58.550 --> 00:39:00.050
And there's also,
what is your team,

00:39:00.050 --> 00:39:01.591
and what does your
organization like?

00:39:01.591 --> 00:39:03.710
If your entire team
is very proficient

00:39:03.710 --> 00:39:06.509
in a particular programming
language, if your company has

00:39:06.509 --> 00:39:09.050
made some decisions about where
they want to focus languages,

00:39:09.050 --> 00:39:11.180
or what's the future of
technology the company,

00:39:11.180 --> 00:39:15.460
that also may affect where
you decide to go for these.

00:39:15.460 --> 00:39:19.627
Because many use cases could
work in one or two of these.

00:39:19.627 --> 00:39:21.710
So on abstractions, just
kind of collecting things

00:39:21.710 --> 00:39:24.650
from throughout the talk,
for Cloud Functions,

00:39:24.650 --> 00:39:27.160
you're thinking about
mostly events and functions.

00:39:27.160 --> 00:39:29.747
App Engine is your code
and the HTTP interactions.

00:39:29.747 --> 00:39:32.080
This is actually kind of the
summary that talk, I guess.

00:39:32.080 --> 00:39:33.663
So we'll just pause
here for a moment.

00:39:33.663 --> 00:39:35.510
All the phones come out.

00:39:35.510 --> 00:39:40.060
And Container Engine
is about applications,

00:39:40.060 --> 00:39:43.940
whole applications, and the
logical architecture of how

00:39:43.940 --> 00:39:45.380
the pieces connect
to each other,

00:39:45.380 --> 00:39:47.588
and being able to run those
in multiple environments.

00:39:47.588 --> 00:39:51.170
And Compute Engine is
your virtualized hardware.

00:39:51.170 --> 00:39:54.450
You think about the actual
machines you've got there.

00:39:54.450 --> 00:39:56.930
So that's abstraction.

00:39:56.930 --> 00:39:59.330
Technical-wise, I
think what tends

00:39:59.330 --> 00:40:03.200
to happen is tech constraints
will kind of pull you further

00:40:03.200 --> 00:40:05.450
down the layer of abstractions.

00:40:05.450 --> 00:40:08.890
And a couple of examples
to talk about this.

00:40:08.890 --> 00:40:11.840
So Cloud Functions has
particular languages

00:40:11.840 --> 00:40:13.850
it works with right
now, particularly,

00:40:13.850 --> 00:40:15.183
in what kind of shape of things.

00:40:15.183 --> 00:40:16.790
If you need more
than that, you're

00:40:16.790 --> 00:40:21.320
going to be in App Engine
or that space for sure.

00:40:21.320 --> 00:40:24.020
Depending upon what language,
whether or not you're

00:40:24.020 --> 00:40:27.350
kind of web facing,
you might actually

00:40:27.350 --> 00:40:29.720
come down a layer beyond that.

00:40:29.720 --> 00:40:34.670
If you need to have hybrid
portability, if you're

00:40:34.670 --> 00:40:37.929
doing protocols beyond kind
of standard web protocols,

00:40:37.929 --> 00:40:40.220
you're going to be in leasing
container, Compute Engine

00:40:40.220 --> 00:40:41.240
kind of space.

00:40:41.240 --> 00:40:44.340
And then if you need like
a very specific kernel,

00:40:44.340 --> 00:40:46.790
you're running Windows today,
you need certain software

00:40:46.790 --> 00:40:49.820
licensing things, you want to
own all of the orchestration

00:40:49.820 --> 00:40:52.850
yourself, you might be
down in Compute Engine.

00:40:52.850 --> 00:40:55.379
So the technical
situation that you're

00:40:55.379 --> 00:40:57.170
in with the problems
you're trying to solve

00:40:57.170 --> 00:40:59.300
may kind of push you in
one place or another.

00:40:59.300 --> 00:41:00.800
And then your team--

00:41:00.800 --> 00:41:04.760
at a very high level, the
higher level abstractions

00:41:04.760 --> 00:41:06.770
are a little bit
more dev focused.

00:41:06.770 --> 00:41:11.504
And as you go down, they tend
to be a bit more ops focused.

00:41:11.504 --> 00:41:12.920
Obviously there's
a lot of overlap

00:41:12.920 --> 00:41:14.086
between that generalization.

00:41:14.086 --> 00:41:17.390
But if you have a team that's
entirely made up of developers,

00:41:17.390 --> 00:41:20.570
you're going to be happier at
higher levels of abstraction.

00:41:20.570 --> 00:41:24.710
And if you have very
specific operations needs,

00:41:24.710 --> 00:41:26.690
those particular
chosen paths might not

00:41:26.690 --> 00:41:29.690
be the best fit for your app.

00:41:29.690 --> 00:41:32.750
And then, I think I kind of hint
at this in the Container Engine

00:41:32.750 --> 00:41:35.240
thing, but where is the future
of your organization going?

00:41:35.240 --> 00:41:37.220
If you know you're
all in on containers,

00:41:37.220 --> 00:41:40.709
that might also push you
towards Container Engine,

00:41:40.709 --> 00:41:41.750
Kubernetes kind of world.

00:41:44.560 --> 00:41:45.857
So keep that in mind.

00:41:45.857 --> 00:41:46.940
What are your people like?

00:41:46.940 --> 00:41:49.670
Where is your
organization going?

00:41:49.670 --> 00:41:51.560
And then, again, just
to come back on--

00:41:51.560 --> 00:41:53.202
this is actually low risk.

00:41:53.202 --> 00:41:55.160
So we spent this whole
time, like half an hour,

00:41:55.160 --> 00:41:58.790
talking about, here's
how to make the decision.

00:41:58.790 --> 00:42:00.690
But it's actually a
fairly low risk decision.

00:42:00.690 --> 00:42:03.830
And I'll try to
convince you of that.

00:42:03.830 --> 00:42:07.070
So one, containers
are really giving us

00:42:07.070 --> 00:42:08.180
a lot of flexibility here.

00:42:08.180 --> 00:42:12.514
So as an example, if you
start in App Engine Flex,

00:42:12.514 --> 00:42:14.930
as a side effect of its normal
operation, when you give it

00:42:14.930 --> 00:42:17.361
the code, it produces
a Docker image.

00:42:17.361 --> 00:42:18.860
You can take that
same Docker image,

00:42:18.860 --> 00:42:20.460
and you can run that in
different environments.

00:42:20.460 --> 00:42:22.010
So you can run
that on Kubernetes.

00:42:22.010 --> 00:42:24.920
And you can run that same
image on Compute Engine.

00:42:24.920 --> 00:42:27.350
You can even run it outside
Google Cloud, other places,

00:42:27.350 --> 00:42:29.960
depending on how many APIs
you're using and the like.

00:42:29.960 --> 00:42:31.970
There might be some
latency effects there.

00:42:31.970 --> 00:42:35.270
But you can run these same
containers kind of anywhere.

00:42:35.270 --> 00:42:38.090
So if you start at a high
level of abstraction,

00:42:38.090 --> 00:42:40.310
it's very easy to kind of
like bump down a level,

00:42:40.310 --> 00:42:41.960
or move down another level.

00:42:41.960 --> 00:42:43.850
So that's cool.

00:42:43.850 --> 00:42:46.100
If you have an
existing system, it

00:42:46.100 --> 00:42:49.850
does take more work to move
up a layer of abstraction.

00:42:49.850 --> 00:42:54.469
You're going to spend some time
investing, doing some learning,

00:42:54.469 --> 00:42:55.260
that sort of thing.

00:42:55.260 --> 00:42:57.000
But you can definitely do that.

00:42:57.000 --> 00:43:01.250
So this isn't a final decision
on where things actually

00:43:01.250 --> 00:43:02.810
run at all.

00:43:02.810 --> 00:43:06.950
And most systems
in the real world

00:43:06.950 --> 00:43:09.590
run their code in
multiple places.

00:43:09.590 --> 00:43:12.350
They might be mostly
Compute Engine based

00:43:12.350 --> 00:43:14.879
with a dash of Functions,
or primarily Function-based,

00:43:14.879 --> 00:43:16.670
but there is some extra
work that they need

00:43:16.670 --> 00:43:18.710
to do in another environment.

00:43:18.710 --> 00:43:20.630
You might be all
in on containers,

00:43:20.630 --> 00:43:22.940
but yeah, there
is that one system

00:43:22.940 --> 00:43:24.380
that nobody knows anymore.

00:43:24.380 --> 00:43:27.650
We're just going to
run that in the VM.

00:43:27.650 --> 00:43:29.529
Or whatever the
situation is, it's

00:43:29.529 --> 00:43:31.070
very likely that
you're going to have

00:43:31.070 --> 00:43:35.300
a mix of different
pieces over time.

00:43:35.300 --> 00:43:38.660
And that's normal,
and probably healthy.

00:43:38.660 --> 00:43:41.374
If you think about, at a
really kind of high level,

00:43:41.374 --> 00:43:43.040
even if they're not
quite microservices,

00:43:43.040 --> 00:43:44.960
but you have different
pieces of your application,

00:43:44.960 --> 00:43:47.330
you're going to run them where
they're the best fit for.

00:43:47.330 --> 00:43:50.640
And so it's very common to see
an API endpoint being an App

00:43:50.640 --> 00:43:53.300
Engine, where some
data processing

00:43:53.300 --> 00:43:56.010
might live somewhere else.

00:43:56.010 --> 00:44:00.550
So that's the TL;DR. And
we'll circle back around.

00:44:00.550 --> 00:44:04.040
The take home, for your
colleagues and other folks,

00:44:04.040 --> 00:44:09.220
is if you are primarily
thinking about events,

00:44:09.220 --> 00:44:13.510
kind of internet glue, HTTP duct
tape, think Cloud Functions.

00:44:13.510 --> 00:44:18.010
If you primarily have code
that is meant to be web-facing

00:44:18.010 --> 00:44:20.140
think App Engine.

00:44:20.140 --> 00:44:24.220
If you are primarily thinking
containerized applications,

00:44:24.220 --> 00:44:26.350
think Kubernetes and
Container Engine.

00:44:26.350 --> 00:44:29.440
And for everything else,
for existing systems,

00:44:29.440 --> 00:44:30.850
start with Compute Engine.

00:44:30.850 --> 00:44:33.450
And think about where the
right fits are for there.

00:44:33.450 --> 00:44:36.500
[MUSIC PLAYING]

